// `Self` in the signature of a method on a GENERIC extension.
//
// `extension Wrap<T> { func same(&self, other: &Self) -> Int }` reports
// ``argument `other` expects `&Self` but got `&Wrap<Int>``. The non-generic
// spelling works (DF-216f, `self_type_signature_positions.saw`); this is the
// case that fix deliberately leaves alone.
//
// Why it is not the same fix: `_ext_self_type` returns an ARGUMENT-FREE `Self`
// for a generic extension — `Wrap`, not `Wrap<T>` — because naming the
// extension's own parameters as arguments makes a payload binding and a `T`
// parameter resolve through different routes to two `T`s that do not unify.
// That spelling works as a RECEIVER, where codegen resolves it through
// `self_type_context`, and cannot work as a written parameter or return type:
// substituting it puts a bare `Wrap` into a signature where only `Wrap$1$Int`
// was ever monomorphized, and the clean error above becomes
// `internal compiler error: Undefined struct: Wrap`. Verified by doing exactly
// that while fixing DF-216f.
//
// So the fix wants `Self` inside a generic extension to carry the
// instantiation, which is the `self_type_context` question rather than the
// substitution question — a ruling, not a patch.
// XFAIL: DF-216r `Self` in the signature of a GENERIC extension's method does not resolve, because that extension's `Self` is argument-free and substituting it would name an unmonomorphized struct
// EXPECT: success
// EXPECT-OUTPUT: same 9

struct Wrap<T> { v: T }

extension Wrap<T> {
    func same(&self, other: &Self) -> Int { 9 }
}

func main() {
    let w = Wrap<Int>(v: 0)
    print("same {w.same(other: &w)}")
}
